<main id="main">

<section class="fe-hero"><div class="fe-w fe-hero-g"><div class="fe-menu"><p class="fe-menu-h">Tonight&rsquo;s menu</p><h1>Dinner, planned with ease.</h1><p class="fe-lead">Feastly is an independent guide to cooking dinner for family and friends. We help you work out how much to cook, when to start and how to balance a plate.</p><dl class="fe-menu-l"><div><dt>To start</dt><dd>Quantities for every guest</dd></div><div><dt>The main</dt><dd>A timeline that ends on time</dd></div><div><dt>To finish</dt><dd>A menu that feels balanced</dd></div></dl><p class="fe-acts"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Plan my dinner</a><a class="fe-ln" href="dinner-party-guide.html">The dinner party guide</a></p></div><figure class="fe-hero-f"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1780304223294-d901e0378bb8?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=720&q=75" alt="roast dinner with gravy and vegetables" width="640" height="720" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Roast, gravy and seasonal vegetables: the heart of a Sunday table.</figcaption></figure></div></section>

<section class="fe-s"><div class="fe-w"><div class="fe-tt"><span>&#10022;</span><h2>A three-course shape that always works</h2><span>&#10022;</span></div><div class="fe-courses"><article><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1606258868842-79eb9eff76af?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=300&q=75" alt="sliced bread on a blue and white plate" width="420" height="300" loading="lazy"><p class="fe-no">I</p><h3>Something to share</h3><p>Bread, dips or a simple soup. Ready before guests arrive, so you can greet them properly.</p></article><article><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1531856396526-a0c8d7bd628b?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=300&q=75" alt="grilled chicken on a table" width="420" height="300" loading="lazy"><p class="fe-no">II</p><h3>One generous main</h3><p>A roast, braise or tray bake with two or three sides that cook alongside it.</p></article><article><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1776530874717-351b45ca4cfe?auto=format&fit=crop&w=420&h=300&q=75" alt="small bowls of food lit by a lantern" width="420" height="300" loading="lazy"><p class="fe-no">III</p><h3>A make-ahead finish</h3><p>Something cold or reheated: fruit, a tart, a set pudding. No last-minute stress.</p></article></div></div></section>

<section class="fe-tl" id="tools"><div class="fe-w"><div class="fe-tt fe-tt-l"><span>&#10022;</span><h2>Three dinner tools</h2><span>&#10022;</span></div><div class="fe-cards">
<div class="fe-card"><div class="fe-card-h"><h3>Dinner Quantity Planner</h3><p>How much to buy and cook.</p></div><div class="fe-card-b"><label for="dq-g">Guests (adults)<input id="dq-g" type="number" min="1" max="40" value="6"></label><label for="dq-k">Children under 12<input id="dq-k" type="number" min="0" max="30" value="0"></label><label for="dq-m">Main<select id="dq-m"><option value="meat">Boneless meat</option><option value="bone">Meat on the bone</option><option value="fish">Fish fillets</option><option value="veg">Vegetarian main</option></select></label><label for="dq-a">Appetites<select id="dq-a"><option value="0.85">Light</option><option value="1" selected>Average</option><option value="1.2">Hearty</option></select></label><div class="fe-out" id="dq-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div>
<div class="fe-card"><div class="fe-card-h"><h3>Cooking Timeline</h3><p>Work back from when you want to eat.</p></div><div class="fe-card-b"><label for="ct-t">Dinner is served at<input id="ct-t" type="time" value="19:30"></label><label for="ct-r">Main needs in the oven (min)<input id="ct-r" type="number" min="10" max="480" step="5" value="90"></label><label for="ct-s">Rest time (min)<input id="ct-s" type="number" min="0" max="60" step="5" value="20"></label><div class="fe-out" id="ct-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div>
<div class="fe-card"><div class="fe-card-h"><h3>Menu Balancer</h3><p>Choose your main; get sides that contrast.</p></div><div class="fe-card-b"><label for="mb-m">Main dish<select id="mb-m"><option value="roast">Roast chicken or meat</option><option value="braise">Rich stew or braise</option><option value="fish">Grilled or baked fish</option><option value="pasta">Baked pasta</option><option value="veg">Vegetable tart or bake</option><option value="curry">Spiced curry</option></select></label><label for="mb-s">Season<select id="mb-s"><option value="warm">Warm months</option><option value="cool" selected>Cool months</option></select></label><div class="fe-out" id="mb-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="fe-s"><div class="fe-w fe-host"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1686489356516-a54af456d168?auto=format&fit=crop&w=560&h=680&q=75" alt="people gathered around a dinner table" width="560" height="680" loading="lazy"></figure><div><div class="fe-tt fe-tt-l"><span>&#10022;</span><h2>The host&rsquo;s countdown</h2></div><ul class="fe-cd"><li><b>3 days before</b><span>Confirm numbers and any dietary needs. Write the shopping list.</span></li><li><b>The day before</b><span>Shop, make dessert, prepare sauces and braises, set the table.</span></li><li><b>Morning of</b><span>Peel and chop vegetables, bring meat out of the fridge in time.</span></li><li><b>2 hours to go</b><span>Main into the oven. Tidy the kitchen as you go.</span></li><li><b>30 minutes to go</b><span>Main out to rest, sides in, plates warming.</span></li><li><b>At the table</b><span>Sit down. The food will wait a minute; your guests came for you.</span></li></ul></div></div></section>

<section class="fe-band"><div class="fe-w fe-band-g"><blockquote>&ldquo;Cook one thing beautifully, and keep everything else simple.&rdquo;<cite>The Feastly rule of hosting</cite></blockquote><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1580554530778-ca36943938b2?auto=format&fit=crop&w=560&h=400&q=75" alt="plated dinner on a white plate" width="560" height="400" loading="lazy"></div></section>

<section class="fe-s"><div class="fe-w"><div class="fe-tt"><span>&#10022;</span><h2>From the guides</h2><span>&#10022;</span></div><div class="fe-gds"><a href="dinner-party-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1661006117166-6227bfc9c8b0?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=400&q=75" alt="people sitting around a table" width="640" height="400" loading="lazy"><div><p class="fe-no">Guide one</p><h3>The Dinner Party Guide</h3><p>Planning a menu, quantities, table setting, and hosting without staying in the kitchen.</p></div></a><a href="cooking-timeline-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1765568741171-71bbaf721747?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=400&q=75" alt="golden yorkshire puddings in a glass dish" width="640" height="400" loading="lazy"><div><p class="fe-no">Guide two</p><h3>The Cooking Timeline Guide</h3><p>Working backwards from serving time so everything lands hot and together.</p></div></a></div></div></section>

<section class="fe-s fe-cream"><div class="fe-w fe-fq"><div class="fe-tt fe-tt-l"><span>&#10022;</span><h2>Kitchen questions</h2></div><div class="fe-faq"><details open><summary>How much meat should I cook per person?</summary><p>For a main course, allow about 150&ndash;200 g of boneless meat or fish per adult, or 250&ndash;350 g if it is on the bone. Add a little more if it is the only substantial dish on the table.</p></details><details><summary>How many dishes should a dinner party have?</summary><p>Three courses is plenty for most hosts: a simple starter, one main with two or three sides, and a dessert. Two courses served generously can be just as welcoming.</p></details><details><summary>How far ahead can I cook?</summary><p>Many braises, stews, sauces and desserts improve when made a day ahead. Roasts, fried food and dressed salads are best done close to serving.</p></details><details><summary>How do I keep everything hot at once?</summary><p>Rest roasted meat under loose foil for 15&ndash;30 minutes while you finish the sides, warm plates in a low oven and serve sides that hold well, such as gratins and roasted vegetables.</p></details><details><summary>What if a guest has a dietary need?</summary><p>Ask when you invite. It is often easiest to make one or two sides suitable for everyone, so no one has a separate plate. Keep labels or ingredient lists for any dish that contains common allergens.</p></details><details><summary>How long should meat rest?</summary><p>As a rule of thumb, small cuts and poultry pieces for 5&ndash;10 minutes, a whole chicken around 15&ndash;20 minutes and a large roast 20&ndash;30 minutes. Always check it has reached a safe internal temperature first.</p></details></div></div></section>

<section class="fe-nl"><div class="fe-w"><div class="fe-nl-c"><h2>The Feastly Card</h2><p>A monthly email with one seasonal menu idea and one timing tip.</p><a class="btn" href="mailto:hello@feastly.com?subject=The%20Feastly%20Card&amp;body=Please%20subscribe%20me.">Subscribe by email</a></div></div></section>

